open the executable wrapper with O_CLOEXEC

Link mode creates the executable wrapper script through a descriptor that is
currently inherited by the compiler and by every process the compiler spawns.
slibtool waits only for the direct compiler child before closing its own copy
and renaming the wrapper into place.  A compiler descendant that is still
alive at that point (for example a linker process that has been reparented)
keeps the wrapper inode open for writing, so a following
`libtool --mode=execute` fails with ETXTBSY ("Text file busy").

This is reproducible with gobject-introspection: g-ir-scanner links its
temporary scanner binary with `libtool --mode=link` and immediately runs
`libtool --mode=execute ldd` on the result.  Under parallel builds the second
step fails intermittently.

The descriptor only needs to be written by the link process itself, so mark it
close-on-exec instead of leaking it to compiler children.

---
diff --git a/src/logic/linkcmd/slbt_linkcmd_executable.c b/src/logic/linkcmd/slbt_linkcmd_executable.c
--- a/src/logic/linkcmd/slbt_linkcmd_executable.c
+++ b/src/logic/linkcmd/slbt_linkcmd_executable.c
@@ -129,7 +129,7 @@
 				dctx->cctx->output) < 0)
 		return SLBT_BUFFER_ERROR(dctx);

-	if ((fdwrap = openat(fdcwd,wrapper,O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC,0644)) < 0)
+	if ((fdwrap = openat(fdcwd,wrapper,O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC|O_CLOEXEC,0644)) < 0)
 		return SLBT_SYSTEM_ERROR(dctx,wrapper);

 	slbt_exec_set_fdwrapper(ectx,fdwrap);
